#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Search-as-you-type field column: keystroke to the first and last frame in which a results region changes.

  flea-field-search calibrate [--delays 0,50,200] [--runs 10]   prove the method against a client with a known delay
  flea-field-search report JSONL                                median [min..max] tables and the self-pair spread
  flea-field-search pickquery DIR [--flat]                      the fixed 6 character query of a column, with what it matches
  flea-field-search explore ENTRANT DIR QUERY [options]         one run with an evidence picture, to learn an entrant's key
  flea-field-search selftest                                    run the detector over built-in frame logs, no display
  flea-field-search preflight                                   name every tool, socket and protocol file a run needs
  flea-field-search measure JSON                                run a plan: see measure()'s docstring for its JSON

The method, in one paragraph. tools/flea-framecap records which presented frames differ from the frame before in a
rectangle (the entrant's window minus the query field), stamped with the compositor's presentation time in
CLOCK_MONOTONIC. Keys are written to the ydotool socket and stamped with CLOCK_MONOTONIC the instant before the write,
so key and frame share one clock and nothing is aligned afterwards. The detector then reads the stamps: the first change
after a key, and the last change before a quiet gap. tests/search-calib.qml is the client that proves it.
Sample input, one recorder line: "F 660389861257350 660389874933379 32 95 126" (presentation ns, received ns, rows).
"""
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import socket
import statistics
import struct
